68
00:04:27,340 --> 00:04:27,560
Avalanche.

69
00:04:28,820 --> 00:04:30,580
And avalanche has to do with snow.

70
00:04:31,220 --> 00:04:44,030
This means that when a mountain that has a lot of snow on it, maybe part of that snow breaks off and starts to roll down, you have a wall of snow that comes down.

71
00:04:44,340 --> 00:04:44,710
That's an avalanche.

72
00:04:45,620 --> 00:04:46,030
That's an avalanche.

73
00:04:46,710 --> 00:04:47,710
So you could say, avalanche.

74
00:04:50,300 --> 00:04:51,300
When the snow comes down.

75
00:04:51,300 --> 00:04:55,150
And it's actually very dangerous because people can get buried under the snow.

76
00:04:55,700 --> 00:04:56,700
Yeah, exactly.

77
00:04:56,890 --> 00:04:57,110
All right.

78
00:04:57,410 --> 00:05:01,390
Another event that has to do with snow is a blizzard.

79
00:05:01,950 --> 00:05:02,470
Okay.

80
00:05:02,700 --> 00:05:06,770
A blizzard is different from an avalanche because an avalanche, maybe it's not snowing.

81
00:05:06,980 --> 00:05:07,670
The snow just falls.

82
00:05:08,900 --> 00:05:13,320
But in a blizzard, you're talking about a storm of snow.

83
00:05:13,320 --> 00:05:15,690
But it's like a lot of snow coming down from the sky.

84
00:05:16,220 --> 00:05:19,070
So much snow that you probably can't see anything.

85
00:05:19,940 --> 00:05:21,090
There's just white everywhere.

86
00:05:21,570 --> 00:05:21,910
Okay.

87
00:05:22,500 --> 00:05:23,500
All right.

88
00:05:23,500 --> 00:05:24,190
So that's a blizzard.

89
00:05:24,500 --> 00:05:26,730
A lot of, lot of snow coming down from the sky.

90
00:05:27,240 --> 00:05:32,620
Now moving on, the opposite of snow and things being wet is we have a drought.

91
00:05:33,260 --> 00:05:35,920
Yeah, this is the opposite of wet actually.

92
00:05:36,220 --> 00:05:38,340
So there's no rain in a drought.

93
00:05:38,760 --> 00:05:41,520
A drought means that things are very dry.

94
00:05:42,000 --> 00:05:43,220
The land is very dry.

95
00:05:43,880 --> 00:05:44,220
Okay.

96
00:05:44,240 --> 00:05:49,740
So it's a period of time that there's no snow, there's no water, there's no rain, there's nothing.

97
00:05:49,940 --> 00:05:50,880
It's very, very dry.

98
00:05:51,240 --> 00:05:52,180
We're going through a drought.

99
00:05:52,540 --> 00:05:52,700
Correct.

100
00:05:52,900 --> 00:05:58,260
And the reason the problem with a drought is very dangerous is because people cannot grow food in a drought.

101
00:05:58,520 --> 00:06:01,000
And so lots of people starve when this happens.

102
00:06:01,200 --> 00:06:01,560
Okay.

103
00:06:01,820 --> 00:06:03,000
So you got to watch out for a drought.

104
00:06:03,600 --> 00:06:12,840
Now, in this case, it not only affected farming, but also we have a hydroelectric power plant that can't work, obviously, because there's no water.

105
00:06:13,280 --> 00:06:15,400
So hydro means water.

106
00:06:16,540 --> 00:06:21,260
Hydroelectric means electricity that is generated by flowing water.

107
00:06:23,060 --> 00:06:27,900
Well, a mill, something that turns water on a river is a way to get power from water.

108
00:06:27,900 --> 00:06:35,460
But a hydroelectric project that can give you electricity is maybe like a dam or an electric mill.

109
00:06:35,480 --> 00:06:35,600
Right.

110
00:06:36,460 --> 00:06:44,340
So usually you have these on rivers and they just take advantage of the flowing of water to turn some huge turbines and generate power.

111
