00:08:17,900 --> 00:08:24,040
Well, a word that many people are unfortunately familiar with is Richter or Richter scale.

133
00:08:25,020 --> 00:08:34,580
And now this is something that a lot of people are talking about right now because of the earthquake in Sichuan, China last year and the earthquake in Haiti very recently.

134
00:08:34,580 --> 00:08:45,200
And so the Richter scale, that's a big R because it's a proper name, is a way for us to determine how severe, how big the earthquake was.

135
00:08:45,500 --> 00:08:45,640
Right.

136
00:08:46,460 --> 00:08:49,220
So people talk about a 6.0, a 7.1.

137
00:08:49,220 --> 00:08:50,700
So that's on the Richter scale.

138
00:08:51,580 --> 00:08:54,640
The lowest is zero and the highest is 10, correct?

139
00:08:54,880 --> 00:08:55,040
Right.

140
00:08:55,540 --> 00:08:57,240
But 10 is like impossible, apparently.

141
00:08:57,680 --> 00:08:57,860
Apparently.

142
00:08:58,700 --> 00:09:04,660
And a lot of them are actually underwater, but the very serious ones are maybe 8.0, 7.0.

143
00:09:04,660 --> 00:09:06,260
All right.

144
00:09:06,260 --> 00:09:07,800
So Richter scale.

145
00:09:08,780 --> 00:09:10,030
Now we talked about earthquakes.

146
00:09:10,500 --> 00:09:12,220
We talked about snowing.

147
00:09:12,220 --> 00:09:13,650
Now let's talk about a volcano.

148
00:09:14,330 --> 00:09:16,610
A volcano in Mexico has erupted.

149
00:09:17,390 --> 00:09:17,730
Okay.

150
00:09:17,930 --> 00:09:19,290
So we've got two words here, volcano.

151
00:09:20,210 --> 00:09:27,370
A volcano is a mountain that has an opening in the center where lava from the middle of the earth, the center of the earth, can come out.

152
00:09:28,070 --> 00:09:30,050
So it's very dangerous, obviously, because it's very hot.

153
00:09:31,150 --> 00:09:32,950
But a volcano doesn't open.

154
00:09:33,260 --> 00:09:34,500
It erupts.

155
00:09:34,500 --> 00:09:38,020
So the verb here that we use with a volcano is to erupt.

156
00:09:38,110 --> 00:09:41,950
Okay, so it doesn't explode, a volcano erupts.

157
00:09:42,030 --> 00:09:44,390
That means to open violently, very quickly.

158
00:09:44,910 --> 00:09:45,270
Okay.

159
00:09:45,510 --> 00:09:54,830
So when this happens, obviously you have a lot of lava, you have maybe snow that melts, and you have floods and landslides.

160
00:09:55,020 --> 00:09:57,460
Okay, landslides.

161
00:09:57,460 --> 00:10:02,020
A landslide is one word, but it's made up of two words that you probably already know.

162
00:10:02,020 --> 00:10:05,370
So land, obviously it's the ground and things like that, the earth.

163
00:10:05,990 --> 00:10:09,230
But a slide is to move down across the ground.

164
00:10:09,750 --> 00:10:18,150
And so a landslide happens when you've got something like lava or water that's pushing all of the earth, all of the ground down.

165
00:10:18,630 --> 00:10:22,750
And it's destroying houses and trees and land, and it's very, very destructive.

166
00:10:23,370 --> 00:10:23,490
Right.

167
00:10:23,770 --> 00:10:32,050
So you usually see this that happens on the side of mountains, on the side of hills, where all that land starts to slide down or to go down.

168
00:10:32,530 --> 00:10:34,350
And it's very similar to an avalanche, right?

169
00:10:34,540 --> 00:10:37,220
But instead of snow falling down, it's land.

170
00:10:37,220 --> 00:10:37,910
Mud and land.

171
00:10:38,220 --> 00:10:39,220
Mud, yeah.

172
00:10:39,220 --> 00:10:40,220
All right.

173
00:10:40,220 --> 00:10:41,500
So we have a landslide.

174
00:10:41,500 --> 00:10:48,430
And because of all the eruptions and the earthquakes, they said that they have a lot of seismic activity.

175
00:10:49,500 --> 00:10:53,530
This word seismic is something that I've heard a lot recently in movies, like 2012.

176
00:10:55,060 --> 00:11:01,470
So seismic means having to do with the movement of the plates under the earth's crust.

177
00:11:01,860 --> 00:11:04,870
So the earth is not everything that we see.

178
00:11:05,100 --> 00:11:08,050
It's also activity, things happening underneath the ground.

179
00:11:08,690 --> 00:11:10,590
And so underneath the ground, we have these big plates.

180
00:11:10,590 --> 00:11:14,710
And seismic activity is what happens when these kind of things start to move.

181
00:11:15,350 --> 00:11:16,210
Activity is movement.

182
00:11:16,820 --> 00:11:25,100
And so seismic activity refers to earthquakes and volcanoes, things that are changing under the ground that will affect us.

183
00:11:25,100 --> 00:11:26,100
OK.

184
00:11:26,100 --> 00:11:27,090
So that's seismic activity.

185
00:11:27,220 --> 00:11:29,060
It's pronounced seismic, right?

186
00:11:29,060 --> 00:11:29,350
Seismic.

187
00:11:30,060 --> 00:11:31,060
That's correct.

188
00:11:31,060 --> 00:11:32,060
Seismic.

189
00:11:32,060 --> 00:11:37,030
And because of all this activity, people say that they may have a tsunami.

190
00:11:37,920 --> 00:11:39,700
Well, this doesn't sound very English, does it?

191
00:11:39,880 --> 00:11:40,840
I know, tsunami.

192
00:11:41,180 --> 00:11:44,500
And actually, if you see the spelling, it's with a T, T-S.

193
00:11:44,900 --> 00:11:45,080
Right.

194
00:11:45,500 --> 00:11:46,900
I think it's a Japanese word, but I'm not positive.

195
00:11:46,900 --> 00:11:48,940
I think so, too.

196
00:11:48,940 --> 00:11:52,440
Tsunami is the name that we give to a giant tidal wave.

197
00:11:52,900 --> 00:11:58,440
So that's a wave of water in the ocean that's very, very dangerous because it can get to 100, 200 feet tall.

198
00:11:59,180 --> 00:12:05,260
And this means that when it comes down, it can destroy houses, cities, cars, things like that.

199
00:12:05,260 --> 00:12:06,260
Right.

200
00:12:06,260 --> 00:12:09,220
And there was a very, very famous tsunami in Southeast Asia in 2004.

201
00:12:09,220 --> 00:12:10,220
Do you remember that?

202
00:12:10,220 --> 00:12:11,220
Yeah, in Indonesia, right?

203
00:12:11,220 --> 00:12:15,060
That's right, in Indonesia and Thailand and Vietnam.

204
00:12:15,060 --> 00:12:16,060
Yeah, it was intense.

205
00:12:16,060 --> 00:12:17,060
It was big.

206
00:12:17,060 --> 00:12:18,700
And it was also because of an earthquake, right?

207
00:12:18,700 --> 00:12:19,190
I think so.

208
00:12:19,700 --> 00:12:21,690
All these earthquakes, they move the earth around.

209
00:12:22,140 --> 00:12:25,370
And then all of a sudden, the reaction is that there's water that moves.

210
00:12:25,500 --> 00:12:28,400
And the water then turned into a wave.

211
00:12:28,660 --> 00:12:33,740
Yeah, just imagine what happens if you have a bucket of water and then you start moving the bucket just a little bit.

212
00:12:34,540 --> 00:12:37,340
You see how water starts to splat all over the place.

213
00:12:37,860 --> 00:12:38,040
That's right.

214
